---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const rtlMarkup = `<!-- One attribute. The framework's CSS is written in LOGICAL properties
     (inline-size, padding-inline, inset-inline-start), so layout mirrors
     without a separate stylesheet. -->
<html lang="ar" dir="rtl">`;

const sgThExamples = `// Every string below was produced by running Intl, not written by hand,
// and a build check re-runs them so an ICU update cannot make this page lie.

new Intl.NumberFormat("en-SG", { style: "currency", currency: "SGD" }).format(1234567.5)
// "$1,234,567.50"   <- a BARE dollar sign

new Intl.NumberFormat("en-SG", { style: "currency", currency: "USD" }).format(1234.5)
// "US$1,234.50"     <- the foreign one gets disambiguated, the local one does not

new Intl.NumberFormat("en-SG", { style: "currency", currency: "SGD",
                                 currencyDisplay: "code" }).format(1234.5)
// "SGD 1,234.50"    <- what a multi-currency column wants

new Intl.NumberFormat("th-TH", { style: "currency", currency: "THB" }).format(1234567.5)
// "฿1,234,567.50"

new Intl.DateTimeFormat("th-TH", { dateStyle: "medium" }).format(new Date("2026-08-17"))
// "17 ส.ค. 2569"    <- the BUDDHIST era: 2569, not 2026

new Intl.DateTimeFormat("th-TH-u-ca-gregory", { dateStyle: "medium" }).format(new Date("2026-08-17"))
// "17 ส.ค. 2026"    <- what an audit trail needs`;

const precisionExample = `// Two DIFFERENT questions, two different answers:
currencyDecimals("IDR")   // 2  — ISO 4217 minor units: what you may ENTER and store
new Intl.NumberFormat("en", { style: "currency", currency: "IDR" })
  .resolvedOptions().maximumFractionDigits   // 0 — CLDR display convention

// Using the display answer to set entry precision silently truncates cents.`;

const numberMarkup = `<!-- Format on the SERVER, in the user's locale, and send the string.
     The framework aligns and spaces it; it never parses or reformats. -->
<td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">1.234,56 €</td>

<!-- Client-side, if you must: -->
new Intl.NumberFormat("de-DE", { style: "currency", currency: "EUR" }).format(1234.56)
new Intl.DateTimeFormat("de-DE", { dateStyle: "medium" }).format(new Date())`;
---
<Gallery title="Internationalization">
  <p class="demo-note">
    ERP screens are dense, and dense screens are where translation
    breaks: German runs ~35% longer than English, Arabic mirrors the
    whole layout, and a date written the American way is a support call
    in Europe. What the framework handles, what it deliberately leaves to
    you, and what to check before shipping a locale.
  </p>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>RTL: one attribute, because the CSS is logical</h2>
    <pre><code>{rtlMarkup}</code></pre>
    <p>The stylesheet uses logical properties throughout —
    <code>inline-size</code>, <code>padding-inline</code>,
    <code>inset-inline-start</code>, <code>text-align: start</code> — so
    a right-to-left document mirrors without a mirrored stylesheet. Six
    places need an explicit flip, because CSS gives no logical form for what
    they use — and all six have one: the
    <a href={base + '/components/form'}>select</a> chevron (a background
    image has no logical position), the
    <a href={base + '/components/tree'}>tree</a> and
    <a href={base + '/components/tree-table'}>tree-table</a> disclosure
    chevrons (a glyph does not mirror — logical properties move boxes, not
    the character inside them), the off-canvas
    <a href={base + '/components/offcanvas'}>drawer</a> slide direction, and
    the <a href={base + '/base/motion'}><code>slide-in-inline-start</code></a>
    animation (<code>transform</code> is physical; there is no logical
    translate), and the
    <a href={base + '/components/data-table'}>data table</a>'s row-state
    stripe and <code>data-tone</code> cell bar (an inset
    <code>box-shadow</code>'s x-offset is physical). The count is gated: a
    seventh unflipped case fails the build rather than shipping a stylesheet
    that mirrors most of the way. Both
    halves of this section were prose until 2026-08-17, and executing them
    found two real defects — the animation slid in from the wrong edge
    despite its logical name, and tree-table was missing the flip its
    sibling tree already had. The sixth was found the same way in 2026-08-23,
    from an owner question about the bar itself: the stripe rendered
    <code>inset 3px 0 0</code> byte-identically in both directions — so it
    sat on the row's TRAILING edge in RTL — while its own forced-colors
    fallback used logical <code>border-inline-start</code> and did flip. Two
    channels for the same marker, disagreeing about which edge means start.
    The gate could not see it, because a <code>box-shadow</code> is not a
    physical box <em>property</em>; it now checks inset x-offsets too.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>Your CSS must follow.</strong> A
    single <code>margin-left</code> in app code re-breaks RTL. Numbers
    stay left-to-right inside RTL text — that is correct, not a bug.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Numbers, currency and dates are your data</h2>
    <pre><code>{numberMarkup}</code></pre>
    <p>The framework <strong>never parses or reformats your values</strong>
    — a deliberate boundary, because a UI that guesses at number formats
    eventually corrupts an amount. What it does provide:
    <code>font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums</code> so digits align in
    columns, right alignment for numeric columns, and — for
    <em>editing</em> — the <a href={base + '/components/money'}>money</a>
    and <a href={base + '/components/quantity'}>quantity</a> fields that
    derive decimal precision from the selected currency or unit, and
    reformat only losslessly (they pad and trim zeros, never round).</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Two rules that prevent the classic bugs:
    format on the server where the locale is known, and keep the machine
    value separate from the display string (that is exactly what
    <code>data-value</code> does on a
    <a href={base + '/components/combobox'}>combobox</a> and what a
    hidden input carries in a form).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Worked examples — Singapore and Thailand</h2>
    <p>Two locales that between them contain most of the traps an ERP hits in
    the region. Every output below was produced by <em>running</em>
    <code>Intl</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>{sgThExamples}</code></pre>
    <div class="bo-alert bo-alert--warning" role="note" style="margin-block: var(--bo-space-4)">
      <p><strong>Thai dates default to the Buddhist era.</strong>
      <code>th-TH</code> resolves to <code>calendar: "buddhist"</code>, so a
      2026 date renders as <strong>2569</strong> — a 543-year offset, in the
      timestamps an audit trail is built on. Ask for
      <code>th-TH-u-ca-gregory</code> wherever the year is a record rather
      than a courtesy. (Thai <em>digits</em> are not a default: the numbering
      system resolves to <code>latn</code>, and opting into
      <code>-u-nu-thai</code> gives <code>฿๑,๒๓๔.๕๐</code>, which no amount
      column should use — it defeats tabular alignment and any parsing.)</p>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">The Singapore case is subtler: in
    <code>en-SG</code>, SGD gets a bare <code>$</code> while USD gets
    <code>US$</code>. That is <code>Intl</code> being correct about the local
    currency, and wrong for a <strong>multi-currency</strong> column, where
    the reader cannot tell which dollar they are looking at. Use
    <code>currencyDisplay: "code"</code> for any column that can hold more
    than one currency — <code>SGD 1,234.50</code> is unambiguous and, being
    the same width for every code, keeps the column aligned.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Entry precision is not display precision</h2>
    <p>The framework's <code>currencyDecimals()</code> (used by the
    <a href={base + '/components/money'}>money field</a> to set an input's
    <code>step</code>) follows <strong>ISO 4217 minor units</strong> — what a
    currency formally subdivides into, and therefore what a user may type and
    the system may store. <code>Intl</code> reports something else: the
    <strong>CLDR display convention</strong>, which drops the minor unit for
    currencies where it exists on paper but not in shops.</p>
    <pre><code>{precisionExample}</code></pre>
    <p>Both are right about their own question, and they disagree for exactly
    seven codes at the time of writing — <code>IQD</code>,
    <code>IDR</code>, <code>HUF</code>, <code>COP</code>, <code>PKR</code>,
    <code>MMK</code>, <code>LAK</code>. Measured, not assumed: a build check
    walks the framework's table against ICU and reports the divergence, so it
    stays a documented list rather than a surprise.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>The rule:</strong> take entry and
    storage precision from ISO minor units (the framework already does), and
    take the rendered string from <code>Intl</code>. Deriving <code>step</code>
    from <code>Intl</code> is the bug this section exists to prevent — on an
    IDR or HUF amount it silently truncates whatever the user typed after the
    decimal point. And <code>JPY</code> really is zero-decimal in both
    answers, so "always two" is wrong even before you reach the edge cases.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>String expansion — the dense-screen killer</h2>
    <ul class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li><strong>Table headers wrap, they do not truncate.</strong> Verify at <em>compact</em> density in your longest locale — that is the combination that breaks first.</li>
      <li><strong>Buttons size to content</strong>, so a long label grows the button rather than clipping. Fixed-width action columns are the anti-pattern.</li>
      <li><strong>Don't build sentences from fragments.</strong> "Approve" + n + "invoices" cannot be translated into languages that inflect; pass the whole sentence with a placeholder.</li>
      <li><strong>Badges and chips are the tightest spaces</strong> — prefer a short status vocabulary you control over free translation.</li>
      <li><strong>Test with a pseudo-locale</strong> (every string ×1.4, accented) before the first real translation lands; it finds clipping without a translator. <strong>This framework does</strong>: a CI gate expands every visible string ~35% with accents, forces compact density, and fails the build if anything clips or the shell scrolls sideways — eight text-dense pages at desktop and phone width. The claims above are measured, not asserted.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Timezones in an audit trail</h2>
    <p>Audit timestamps are legal records. Store UTC, render in the
    viewer's zone, and <strong>say which zone you rendered</strong> —
    "2026-08-16 15:02 CET" is unambiguous; "15:02" is not. Use a real
    <code>&lt;time datetime="…"&gt;</code> element so the machine-readable
    instant travels with the display string, and keep a fixed format in
    audit trails rather than relative time ("2 hours ago" is unusable in
    a dispute).</p>
  </section>

  <Related links={[
    ['/components/money', 'Money field'],
    ['/components/quantity', 'Quantity'],
    ['/components/date', 'Date'],
    ['/base/print', 'Print & reports'],
  ]} />
</Gallery>
